		{
			"id":"db891a56fd5d9f28",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# What is Excel?\n\n- IDE\n- Functional programing environment\n- Standard way to exchange data\n\n\nHow many Excels do we have?\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-640,
			"y":-440,
			"width":520,
			"height":340,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"98407dd6e56c0f6f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Who invented spreadsheets?\n\nIn 1979, the two students invented **VisiCalc** after getting frustrated with homework in a business class, which led them to develop a radically new solution.\n\nOn the link below you can try the original VisiCalc online in an emulator. Note that the system RAM was only 64 KB :) \n\nhttps://www.pcjs.org/software/pcx86/app/other/visicalc/1981/\n\n\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-640,
			"y":-60,
			"width":520,
			"height":340,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"3ec72cb6e4a932ce",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# What happened to VisiCalc? \n\nMeanwhile, Lotus 1-2-3 appeared and quickly took over VisiCalc's market position. In 1985, Lotus Development Corporation acquired Software Arts (and with it, the rights to VisiCalc), and almost immediately pulled the product from the market, since it would have competed with its own Lotus 1-2-3 software.\n\nQ: Could the idea have been protected by a patent? ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-80,
			"y":-60,
			"width":520,
			"height":340,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"ad7fbb901bdedb11",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Joel Spolsky\n\nhttps://www.joelonsoftware.com/",
			"styleAttributes":{},
